From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: socfpga: de10nano: reserve 1 MiB of ramoops/pstore
 at 0x1FE00000

Adds a /reserved-memory ramoops node so that a crash -- or a hang that
produces no crash at all -- leaves a record in DRAM that the next boot
can read.

Rationale
---------
Hardware finding H-1 (Wave-1 pass, three confirmed events): a read of an
lwhps2fpga offset that no fabric slave decodes hard-hangs the HPS.  There
is no bus fault, no exception, no panic, and -- confirmed on the third
event with a serial console attached and capturing -- not one byte of
console output.  Three hangs produced three empty logs.  The finding's
own conclusion was that "one DT node would have said all of this on the
first event".  This is that node.

The channel that failed was the UART: it stops being serviced at the
instant of the hang, so everything printk had produced but not yet
drained died with it, and everything the test tool knew about what it was
about to do was never written down anywhere durable.  A ramoops region is
a channel that does not need a live CPU to be read: it is written as the
messages are produced, into DRAM, and read back after the reset.

What it actually buys, stated precisely
---------------------------------------
For an H-1-class hang there is no kmsg dump to take.  The kernel never
reaches its die path, kmsg_dump() is never called, and the "dmesg"
records this node provisions stay empty for exactly the failure that
motivated it.  Overclaiming that would be worse than not having the node.

What does survive is:

  * the CONSOLE area -- everything printk emitted up to the hang,
    including the tail still sitting in the UART FIFO, because
    pstore's console frontend is written on the printk path and not on
    the crash path; and

  * the PMSG area -- whatever userspace wrote to /dev/pmsg0 before the
    fatal access.  A probe tool that writes "about to read LW +0x14"
    before it issues the read converts a silent brick into a named
    offset, which is the single fact all three H-1 investigations spent
    their time recovering by bisection instead.

The dmesg records are for the ordinary oops and panic this board can also
produce, which today are equally unrecoverable if the board does not get
far enough to write them to the card.  Both jobs are wanted; that is why
the region is split three ways rather than given whole to either.

Address: 0x1FE00000, and why it is the only comfortable one
-----------------------------------------------------------
A reserved-memory region has to live inside memory the kernel actually
owns, and on this board that is a narrow window (docs/boot-chain.md
section 6.4 carries the arithmetic):

  0x1FF00000   the mem=511M cap.  early_mem() discards every memory
               region the DTB advertised and adds back exactly
               [0, 0x1FF00000).  The token comes from stock U-Boot's
               CONFIG_BOOTARGS, which this tree does not own.
  0x1FFFF000   MiSTer's shmem mailbox (Main_MiSTer fpga_io.cpp:398).
  0x1FFFFF08   MiSTer's warm-boot flag.
  0x20000000   the 512 MiB DDR3 aperture shared with the FPGA over
               f2sdram (patch 0044's mister_ddr_aperture).

Everything at or above the cap is therefore already spoken for -- by
MiSTer userspace or by the FPGA -- and none of it is memory the kernel is
permitted to reserve in the first place.  That leaves [0, 0x1FF00000),
and within it the top is the only placement that costs nothing: a region
in the middle of lowmem punches a hole that the linear map has to work
around, whereas a region at the top merely shortens lowmem by 1 MiB.

0x1FE00000 is 2 MiB-aligned, which is PMD_SIZE on ARM without LPAE, so
the linear map ends on a clean PMD boundary instead of degenerating into
page mappings around a hole.  Size 0x100000 puts the end of the region
exactly on the cap, with no gap to explain.

Verified free rather than assumed free, at the time of writing:

  * no CMA area exists -- CONFIG_DMA_CMA is not set in this tree, so
    dma_contiguous_reserve() reserves nothing and cannot land here;
  * the only other static reservation on this SoC is
    socfpga_cyclone5.dtsi's /memreserve/ of the first 4 KiB, for the
    secondary-core trampoline;
  * MiSTer_fb (patch 0001) takes its memory from the FPGA aperture via
    its DT reg, not from kernel RAM;
  * no Main_MiSTer shmem_map() call can land in this range: every call
    site resolves to the 0x1FFFF000 mailbox page or into the aperture --
    the 0x22000000 framebuffer (video.cpp FB_ADDR), the scaler window at
    0x20000000 (scaler.h MISTER_SCALER_BASEADDR), savestate bases
    range-checked against [0x20000000, 0x40000000) (user_io.cpp), and
    fpga_mem() addresses masked into 0x20000000+ (shmem.h).

The split, and why these four numbers
-------------------------------------
ramoops carves the region in probe order -- dmesg from the base, then
console, then pmsg, then ftrace (fs/pstore/ram.c ramoops_probe) -- so
1 MiB becomes:

  0x1FE00000  +0x0B8000  dmesg    23 records x 0x8000   (record-size)
  0x1FEB8000  +0x040000  console  256 KiB               (console-size)
  0x1FEF8000  +0x008000  pmsg      32 KiB               (pmsg-size)
  0x1FF00000             == the cap; nothing left over.

The dmesg area is whatever remains after the fixed areas
(dump_mem_sz = size - console - ftrace - pmsg), divided into record-size
chunks: 0x100000 - 0x40000 - 0x8000 = 0xB8000, and 0xB8000 / 0x8000 = 23
exactly, so the region divides with no remainder.  Twenty-three retained
records is deep enough that a reboot loop does not immediately overwrite
the first and most informative crash.

  record-size 0x8000 (32 KiB) must be a power of two: ramoops silently
    applies rounddown_pow_of_two() otherwise, which would waste nearly
    half the dmesg area without logging anything.  32 KiB is sized
    against PSTORE_DEFAULT_KMSG_BYTES, which caps one kmsg dump at 10240
    bytes -- so a full dump fits with 3x headroom before PSTORE_COMPRESS
    deflates it, and a record is never what truncates a crash.

  console-size 0x40000 (256 KiB) is the largest share, because it is the
    area that pays off for a silent hang.  It is a circular buffer of
    everything going to the console, written as printk produces it, so it
    holds the tail of the boot plus whatever has run since -- on the
    order of a couple of thousand lines at typical MiSTer line lengths,
    which spans a boot and a test run comfortably.  This is the area that
    would have had something to say on all three H-1 events.

  pmsg-size 0x8000 (32 KiB) is /dev/pmsg0, read back after the reset as
    /sys/fs/pstore/pmsg-ramoops-0.  Deliberately small: it is for "which
    offset am I about to touch", not for logging, and its cost on the
    hazardous path is one write().

  ftrace-size 0 is stated rather than omitted, so the four numbers in the
    node can be checked against the arithmetic above without knowing the
    binding's defaults.  PSTORE_FTRACE is deliberately not set even though
    it would resolve -- the shared linux.config already carries
    FUNCTION_TRACER=y and DEBUG_FS=y, and PSTORE_FTRACE only depends on
    them (it selects nothing), so "it would drag in the tracer" is not the
    reason and would be false in this tree.  The reason is the budget: a
    fourth zone would shrink the three areas above, to serve
    per-function-call tracing -- a job unrelated to H-1.  At 0, ramoops
    allocates no zone and does not register the frontend.

  ecc-size is absent (0) on purpose.  Reed-Solomon ECC costs CPU on every
    record write and defends against bit rot in DRAM that lost refresh.
    It does not defend against the failure that matters here -- whether
    the last stores reached DRAM at all -- and enabling it would change
    the buffer layout and shrink all three areas.

  max-reason 3 is KMSG_DUMP_EMERG (include/linux/kmsg_dump.h): dump on
    panic, oops and emergency restart, but not on KMSG_DUMP_SHUTDOWN (4).
    The default when the property is absent is 2 (KMSG_DUMP_OOPS).
    Raising it to 3 costs nothing and catches the emergency-restart path;
    raising it to 4 or 5 would write a record on every clean shutdown and
    rotate real crashes out of a 23-deep ring inside a day of ordinary
    use.

Memory type, and the alias question
-----------------------------------
mem-type is absent, which is 0, "write-combined".  Because the region is
inside the kernel's memory map, pfn_valid() is true for it and
persistent_ram_buffer_map() takes the vmap() path with
pgprot_writecombine() (fs/pstore/ram_core.c).  That is the right
attribute and both alternatives are wrong here: mem-type 2 ("cached")
would leave the newest records in the A9 D-cache at the moment the CPU
wedges, losing precisely the last lines -- the ones worth having --
while mem-type 1 ("unbuffered", strongly ordered on ARMv7) would
serialise every console byte to buy ordering against a device that does
not exist.

That leaves a cacheable alias of these pages in the kernel linear map,
which is accepted here rather than overlooked.  It is NOT the
mismatched-attribute hazard that patch 0044's header argues about at
length, and the difference is worth being explicit about because the two
look alike:

  * 0044's hazard is a write-combining USER mapping of pages that are
    also ordinary allocatable RAM the kernel writes through its cacheable
    linear mapping.  Two writers, two attributes, same lines.
  * Here the region is reserved before the allocator ever sees it, so
    nothing writes it through the linear alias: not the buddy allocator,
    and not memtest (CONFIG_MEMTEST is not set in this tree, and
    early_memtest() iterates free ranges only in any case).  A line the
    CPU speculatively fills through the alias is clean, and a clean line
    cannot be written back over a record.

no-map was considered and rejected.  On ARM it would remove the linear
alias -- map_lowmem() iterates with for_each_mem_range(), whose flags do
not include MEMBLOCK_NOMAP, so should_skip_region() skips the region --
and adjust_lowmem_bounds() uses the same iterator, so arm_lowmem_limit
and high_memory would shorten correctly rather than dangle.  What it
would NOT do is move ramoops to the ioremap path: arch/arm's pfn_valid()
answers from memblock.memory, and NOMAP regions stay in memblock.memory,
so the mapping ramoops ends up making is byte-for-byte the same either
way.  So no-map buys only the removal of an alias that is provably
write-never, at the price of departing from every in-tree ARM ramoops
node (all of which are plain reserved-memory).  Not worth it here.  It
would be worth revisiting if this region ever moved into the middle of
lowmem, where the linear-map split is real rather than a shortening.

Persistence, and the limit that motivates the watchdog
------------------------------------------------------
DRAM keeps its contents across a warm reset, and neither U-Boot nor the
kernel writes this range on the way back up: U-Boot loads the zImage at
0x01000000 and relocates itself to the top of the full 1 GiB, both far
from 510 MiB, and the kernel reserves the region before the allocator
runs.

A POWER CYCLE is a different thing.  The DDR3 stops being refreshed and
the contents decay over seconds.  H-1 hangs were power-cycle-only
recoveries, so for that exact failure this node yields a best-effort
record and not a guaranteed one.  That is not an argument against the
node -- a best-effort record beats three empty logs -- but it is the
argument for having a watchdog capable of turning such a hang into a warm
reset, where the record is reliable.  docs/rt-beta-kernel.md section 9
carries that open question and the on-device test that would settle it.

Userspace
---------
Nothing appears by itself.  The records are exposed by pstorefs:

  mount -t pstore pstore /sys/fs/pstore

after which dmesg-ramoops-N, console-ramoops-0 and pmsg-ramoops-0 can be
read.  Records persist until removed with rm, which is what frees the
slot for the next crash -- a harvest step that forgets to delete will
eventually stop recording new ones.

Binding, and why this patch is beta-local
-----------------------------------------
Unlike 0043 and 0044, this node uses only documented upstream bindings:
compatible = "ramoops" under /reserved-memory, exactly as
Documentation/devicetree/bindings/reserved-memory/ramoops.yaml describes,
and the platform device is created by of_platform_default_populate_init()
which matches "ramoops" from its reserved_mem_matches table
(drivers/of/platform.c).  Nothing here is invented.

It still lives only in linux-patches-beta/ and is listed only in that
directory's series, for the same reason 0043-0045 do: the shared
linux-patches/ directory has no series file and is applied whole to the
stock 6.18 build via BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_PATCH, so a copy there would put
this node into the stock DTB -- where CONFIG_PSTORE is not set, no driver
claims the region, and the only effect would be 1 MiB of RAM reserved
from every stock user for nothing.  Beta-local is what keeps the stock
build byte-identical.

The four config symbols it needs (CONFIG_PSTORE, CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM,
CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE, CONFIG_PSTORE_PMSG) are added to
board/mister/de10nano/linux-rt.fragment in the same change, each with its
justification, and are beta-only for the same reason.

Provenance
----------
Origin:       None.  Originally authored for this tree; no MiSTer fork
              commit corresponds to it.
Author:       Michael C. Ferguson, 2026-08-14.
Upstream:     No.  The binding is upstream and the node is
              schema-correct, but the content is board policy, not a
              hardware description: it encodes a 1 MiB carve-out whose
              legality depends on a mem=511M token supplied by a U-Boot
              this tree does not own, and whose size split is tuned to
              one project's debugging needs.  Upstream socfpga has no
              reason to carry either.  Disposition "carry"
              (docs/patch-provenance.md 3.1).
Depends on:   0004 (the DTS base) only.  It anchors on the memory@0 node,
              which no other patch in this series touches, and its hunk
              sits above the region 0043 and 0044 add to, so it applies
              at the same line whether or not those two are present.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
